content:
Reducer operations receive the state of **their own scope**, already unwrapped. Inside the reducer you write `state.items`. From outside, the same data is `doc.state.global.items`.

```ts
// document-models/todo/src/reducers/todos.ts
addTodoOperation(state, action) {
  state.items.push({ id: action.input.id, title: action.input.title, done: false });
}
```

```ts
// from a test or an editor
expect(doc.state.global.items).toHaveLength(1);
```

The mistake this prevents is writing `state.global.items` inside the reducer. Under `strict` TypeScript that fails to compile, which is the good case — but when the state type is loose or the value is threaded through an `unknown`, you instead get a runtime `undefined` and a reducer that silently does nothing while the operation still lands in the log.

The corollary matters as much as the rule: a reducer **cannot** see or touch the other scope. A global-scope operation has no access to local state and vice versa. If a transition genuinely needs to read both, that is a signal the data is in the wrong scope, or that the work belongs in the editor (which can read both) before dispatch.

The scoping is not inferred at call time — it is bound when the operation is specified and baked into the generated action creator, which passes `"global"` or `"local"` to `createAction`. The reducer signature is simply the other half of that contract.

Verified in generated project code: reducers in `document-models/<name>/v1/src/reducers/*.ts` assign directly to `state.<field>` with no scope hop.

_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/02-Learn/03-document-models/04-your-first-document-model.mdx (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._
